Yes. You can tell a myth that remembers how to solve a problem. Give each character a rule they live by, then watch what happens when those rules meet.

Say a village has one lantern and four people who need to cross a bridge at night. At most two can cross at once, and every crossing needs the lantern. The four take 1, 2, 7, and 10 minutes to cross. A pair moves at the slower person’s pace.

The one-minute runner figures he should escort everybody. He can do it in 21 minutes. Then the two slowest look at each other and say, “We can go together.”

The two fastest cross first. The runner brings the lantern back. The two slowest cross together. The two-minute traveler brings it back. Then the two fastest cross again.

2 + 1 + 10 + 2 + 2 = 17 minutes.

You could tell the story of that night without ever writing the equation, and someone listening might still carry away the move. The two longest crossings can happen in the same ten minutes. Then you can hand that person a completely different problem and ask, “Who are the two slow travelers here, and can they cross together?”

That’s the constellation part. You can forget everybody’s name and still recognize the arrangement when you see it again.